From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Will Faust <willtechnoduck@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 18:21:03 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll/arm64ec: fix x64 setjmp/longjmp by unwinding a PE pc,
 not a JIT-pool pc

do_setjmpex() called unwind_one_frame() with context.Rip still a JIT-pool VA.
Unwind info is registered for PE VAs, so RtlLookupFunctionEntry found no
function entry, RtlVirtualUnwind treated the frame as a leaf, popped a zero
return address, and left Rip = 0. Because RtlIsEcCode(0) is false, the
"caller is x64, use its context instead of the ARM one" branch was taken and
copied that all-zero context over the registers NTDLL__setjmpex had just
stored correctly. NTDLL_longjmp -- which on ARM64EC has no
if (!buf->Frame) fast path -- then called RtlUnwind(0, 0, ...), raising
STATUS_LONGJUMP with a target of RIP 0, and the guest branched to address 0.

Two changes:

1. unwind_one_frame() reverse-translates the control pc through
   xlate_ios_jit_rev() before both RtlLookupFunctionEntry and
   RtlVirtualUnwind, mirroring what virtual_unwind() already does. This is
   safe for addresses that were never pool-mapped: xlate_ios_jit_rev()
   resolves by registered alias identity and returns its input unchanged
   when there is no PE counterpart, rather than guessing from address ranges.

2. unwind_one_frame() now reports whether it actually unwound, and the
   copy-back requires unwound && context.Rip && !RtlIsEcCode(context.Rip).
   A non-zero Rip alone is not proof of a successful unwind, and a failed
   unwind must never overwrite a good jump buffer.

Verified on device against LibreMines (Qt5). Before: UNWOUND Rip=0x0 ->
COPY-BACK (treated as x64 caller), FINAL Rip=0, process dies with
c0000005 at pc=0 after 22s. After: UNWOUND ok=1 Rip=0x71F8E9BA71 ->
COPY-BACK (real x64 caller); that address is Qt5Gui.dll+0x20ba71, exactly
frame [5] of the original crash's guest call chain. Zero SEGVs at pc=0,
zero "REFUSING low/invalid RIP=0x0", zero c0000005 terminations, one clean
STATUS_LONGJUMP, ~15 minutes of run time.

This is not specific to one application. Any x86-64 guest that uses
setjmp/longjmp hits it, which includes every libjpeg and libpng error path.

Also adds two diagnostics, both self-calibrating and capped:

- [setjmp-life] in do_setjmpex/NTDLL_longjmp, which is what located this and
  now doubles as a regression canary (it prints UNWOUND ok=).
- [iat-life] in loader.c: reports an import slot that is NULL after binding,
  and sweeps every loaded module's import table after each LdrLoadDll,
  checking both the PE view and the JIT-pool copy. It emits a per-sweep
  coverage beacon so silence is evidence rather than ambiguity. This refuted
  an earlier theory that the crash came from an unbound VCRUNTIME140!longjmp
  slot: 52 sweeps x ~13,800 slots reported no NULL in either view.

d3d9: one-shot [d3d9-probe] on the three Direct3DCreate9 entry points, to
tell an import-table-only reference apart from real d3d9 use.

(cherry picked from commit 25b00c4700ee1c17594247b6e0c7e16239bba9f1)
